# The Myth of the Twin Rivers

In the beginning, two rivers flowed down from opposite sides of the Great Mountain—the River of Silver and the River of Gold. Each river possessed magic: Silver could heal wounds, Gold could grow abundant harvests. But neither alone could sustain life in the valley below.

The River Spirits met at the mountain's base, where their waters nearly touched.

"If I pour my waters into yours," said Silver, "they will triple in power. But you must choose how much to return to my banks."

Gold hesitated. "And if you share with me, the same? We could both grow strong—or one could take everything."

For three days and nights they watched each other, their peoples suffering below. Some villagers whispered, "Hold back, protect what's yours." Others pleaded, "Trust, and abundance will flow."

Finally, Silver poured generously into Gold's stream. The waters swirled, tripled, and shimmered with new power. Gold faced the choice: keep all the bounty, or honor the trust given?

Gold remembered the suffering below and the courage Silver showed. She returned even more than received, and Silver did the same. Their mingled waters became the River of Stars, and the valley bloomed eternal.

The spirits learned: trust multiplied becomes abundance, but only when reciprocity flows both ways.
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

